@@ -268,4 +269,48 @@ I'd say that we don't do the sin offerings precisely because of the Book of Hebr
 There is no principle that has changed in worship from the Old Testament to the New. Christ has fulfilled the sin offering sacrifices, which is why we no longer use those offerings. Incense, on the other hand (as one example), is a symbol of prayer. Because prayer has not fundamentally changed, we continue with the usage of incense in relationship to worship. However, even ignoring that argument (which I think is an important argument), we look at Malachi 1:11 and see that the Prophet Malachi tells us that there is going to be incense in the New Testament Church's worship. 
 
 I'm looking at worship as, "This is what we've received, and unless there is a component of worship we have that violates a principle of Scripture, we are going to maintain it." So I would need a positive case to doubt what we have received.
-To add to those last two sentences: When I think of the Regulative Principle, I don't think of it as a deconstruction of our worship. I actually do not think that deconstruction is a safe endeavor, spiritually. So when I see practice XYZ I don't ask myself, "Is it logically possible that this is wrong or can be wrongly interpreted? If so, we must abandon it." I, rather, need a positive case for why it is a violation of Scripture's principles of worship. I see this in one of the examples I gave in class: Joshua 7:6. The Second Commandment tells us that we should not bow down to, nor worship, images. Yet Joshua and the Elders of Israel did prostrate before the Ark of the Covenant (without God saying to do so), which has two cherubim on it. Many proponents of certain forms of the Regulative Principle of Worship today would look at this as idolatry if they were standing there and witnessing it. Yet God does not judge or condemn for this action; instead, He accepts it.
