+### 8/24/26, 12:22 PM — M1B3AU
+
+I wanted to say this last night, but I did not want to interrupt Father James because I’m not the teacher, nor am I ordained. However, something I would like to point out that is extremely important is the idea that Christ has redeemed the universe. As we see all over the New Testament, especially in Romans and Hebrews, is that Christ is the fulfillment of many people from the Old Testament, possibly the most important of which is Adam. Christ is making all things new.  I whispered this point to Father Brian as not to interrupt. 
+
+Let’s go back to the paschal lamb. Did they eat the lamb? Yes, they ate the lamb. However, the lamb was dead. Christ is the direct fulfillment of the eating of the lamb, and is a far superior sacrifice because he does not need to be sacrificed multiple times; and when he is consumed, he is not dead, nor does he die. He is alive. He is superior because he is alive and does not need to be re-sacrificed. In taking communion, we are partaking of his divine nature. God is not just going to make all things new eventually, he has already started the process. 
+
+Father Brian hit the nail on the head last night when he was talking about the purpose of the 39 articles. The purpose is not some sort of doctrine statement to define everything down to the last bit like the Roman Catholic catechism, the Westminster Confession of Faith, or the London Baptist Confession of Faith. The purpose is to unite Christianity around a minimum standard of what is to be believed. The Orthodox Church takes a much more Mystic approach on doctrinal issues like this because they don’t want to overly define something, and then box themselves into a particular systematic theology. I think Martin Luther did very well with this in his view on communion/the Eucharist. We have to be careful over defining everything. All we know is that we are partaking of Christ’s divine nature when we take the bread and the wine because of his one sacrifice.
